{"kind":"report","text":"READY-FOR-REVIEW at exact head 55595cb364b423430d3b87e95870243e1e2b83d5 on branch task/world-basis, artifact 7aa29de8f738389bb85d9d5fc963e379100c61c5.\n\nAgainst the conditions of the request. The convention is decided and ratified rather than settled in passing: proposal a47da8f3a28d0ae8ae92d06c9b357368b20533ed, ratified by hugh at d188bee146075d4dd7afe2cfdbbf7812ccc06d22. A statement describing behaviour rests on the artifacts for that behaviour, and whoever changes behaviour supersedes the prior artifact for the same path. The projector surfaces describes-a-superseded-world distinctly from ordinary staleness, and marks artifacts with an empty rests_on as unable to flare. SKILL.md discipline 8 and the AGENTS.md loop are updated to match what landed. The alternative the request allowed — recording that this class of invalidation stays a human vigilance duty — was not taken.\n\nGates actually run on that exact head: go vet clean, the full go test suite passing, gofmt clean for every file this branch touches. Six new tests, each verified mutation-sensitive by breaking the implementation and confirming a named test fails; one mutation initially reported no failures because it broke compilation instead, so it was re-run in a form that compiles. The negative case rides the existing golden fixture, where a retired request makes an artifact stale without the world having moved.\n\nWhat I did not do, stated rather than left to be found. Paths compare as exact strings, so an artifact recorded at a comma-joined path does not warn about a predecessor at one of its members, and this change is itself such a case; the proposal chose exact matching deliberately, because inferring that two spellings mean the same tree is guesswork and would retroactively change how existing history projects. The reference documentation does not yet describe the three marks; that belongs to the documentation set request, which this convention was sequenced ahead of precisel